Z||000000||88/2.8||03-08-2001||10:45 AM||||I've been planning to upgrade my fiero this summer with some of the various "bolt-on" performance items.Here is the list:<P>Hypertech street runner-fiero store-100$<BR>Hypertech thermo.master-fiero store-115$<BR>Hypertech 176 degree fan switch-fiero store-45$<BR>Hypertech power stat 160 degree thermostat-fiero store-17$<BR>Converted flowmaster "40" series muffler-cwestco-140$<BR>Free flow cat.-cwestco-80$<BR>Thats basically it, i will put headers on it from cwestco also if i can come up with the money, but this is all for now.<P>Is this a good idea???<P>My Cars:<BR>88 V6 Formula Fiero<BR>79 Formula Firebird 400 4spd.||||reg||6
Z||000001||c m west||03-08-2001||10:47 AM||||the one problem you will run into with a colder thermostat is that the computer will sense that the engine is not getting up to operating temperature so it will continue to add more fuel... so to sum it up, more fuel=poor milage=hesitation<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||88/2.8||03-08-2001||10:54 AM||||How could i stop that problem while still using the same parts?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||03-08-2001||10:56 AM||||just use the factory rated thermostat - afterall, the factory put that temperature rating in there for a reason<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||c m west||03-08-2001||10:57 AM||||another problem that I forsee is that you are installing a 176 degree fan switch, this will mean that your fan will be on nearly all of the time, from experience, these motors do not hold up under constant running<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||88/2.8||03-08-2001||11:01 AM||||I would do that but the chip requires the use of the cooler thermostat. My engine runs kinda hot anyways and i have heard that the hypertech cooling system works pretty well. I did want to ask you about the velocity tube that u have on your site, are there any problems with dirt finding its way through the intake???<P><BR>BTW, your fiero website is awesome!!!||||reg||7
Z||000006||Alex4mula||03-08-2001||01:32 PM||||That's why you need a complementary PROM chip when you use a lower temp stat. The new code will compensate for the lower temps (i.e. running rich).||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||03-08-2001||02:34 PM||||you don't HAVE to have the different thermostat. i had the "thermo-master" with a stock stat and never had a problem.. just like on the box.. "not for use on emission controlled vehicles or cars with cats" well mine passed emissions just fine. I reprogrammed my own chip for Nitrous and larger fuel injectors which realized hella gains both on and off the juice. and TERRIBLE gas milage... but it was faster!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> unless your running a S/C or nitrous don't drop the $ for headers. you'll loose low end torque. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6headerdyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6headerdyno.jpg</A>  i had all kinda of feul management and timing adjustmetn and those are the best numbers i could muster out of the headers. the 11+lbft loss of TQ REALLY hurt the car performance.. and the shift of the hp/tq band isn't really all that great unless all you do is autocross. try and stick with a single in single out 2.5" exhaust. the cross flow units disrupt the flow and yeah you'll gin HP and TQ but not as much as you would if you used a singe in/out unit. those are my dyno findings... in stead of the chip.. get an MAS 6A or AL. and Crane PS or LX 91-92 coil. <P>just my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||03-08-2001||03:30 PM||||Aftermarket chips aren't worth it. GM does a good job with engine management.||||reg||1
Z||000009||c m west||03-08-2001||04:33 PM||||as far as chips go they are only as good as the variable that gets changed by each manufacturer - one could modify the fuel curve, while another would compensate with timing per say - as far as chips go you want something that gets rid of the rev limiter - PERIOD....<P>.... as far as anything getting into the velocity tube - it just doesnt work that way, it replaces the stock restrictive unit that draws air from the same source - it is up to the filter to trap these things<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Carrolles||03-08-2001||05:35 PM||||You've listed almost $500 worth of parts excluding the headers.  Why not put up a few more dollars and find a good used 3.4L Camaro engine, port the intake and exhaust system, and upgrade the ignition box.  You'll get way more torque than you'll get from these mods on a 2.8L.||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||03-09-2001||02:58 PM||||From what I have seen and talked to people about Hypertech I have not seen anything concrete enough for me to put down that kind of money for thermostats or chips. I have a manual fan switch and stock thermostat. Sitting in traffic I switch it on and not worry about it getting anywhere near 230 degrees...<P>However, the few times I have forgotten about it and got up to freeway speeds with the fan on the temp goes down a bit more and my gas mileage does suffer.<P>If the basic theory is that a colder engine runs better I would have to disagree. More cold air going into the intake, not cold engines make more efficient power. The gas mileage penalty just isn't worth it (for me at least!) for day to day driving or even long trips!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||03-10-2001||08:40 PM||||Performance chips:<BR>There are only a few common things done to chips, and none of them are all that effective:<BR>1) The most common is to widen the block learn multiplier limits. This is a bandaid that extends the ability of the stock calibration to work with highly modified engines. If your engine isn't highly modified, this won't help. At all.<P>2) Calbration for 160 thermostat: the claimed benefits of 160 degree thermostat operation and ecm calibration to match are bogus. You may in fact hurt your engine, as the oil won't get up to proper operating temperature and lose its viscosity.<P>3) Another common change is to remove the EGR function. Doesn't the EGR valve close at full throttle? Removing EGR really doesn't do anything except make you fail emissions.<P>4) Removing the rev limiter on an engine with stock valve springs and any significant number of miles is just a bad idea. The rev limiter is there for a reason. Why disable the rev limiter at all since the stock cam and intake combo start running out of breath at 5500 or so anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000013||JC Webb||03-10-2001||10:02 PM||||A MSD 6 AL was mentioned above.  I plan on buying one soon.  I checked the archives and found a thread about having to re-program the MSD for the V-6 firing order.  No one could come to a conclusion on whether or not to have it reprogramed on that thread.  Should you have the MSD re-programmed? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||03-10-2001||10:10 PM||||I couldn't imagine anyone changing the BLM limits to solve a fueling problem. If they know where the limits are, but certainly know where the VE and base fueling is. ||||reg||5
Z||000015||Will||03-11-2001||12:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>I couldn't imagine anyone changing the BLM limits to solve a fueling problem. If they know where the limits are, but certainly know where the VE and base fueling is. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is what I had heard was one of the things done for "generic" chips. I wouldn't do that on my car, and you wouldn't do that on your car, but a company trying to make a product to work with dozens of different engines in varying states of tune would take that shortcut. Jack of all trades, master of none.||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||03-11-2001||02:26 AM||||I see your point Will. In that sense your right. It's a less than optimal solution (more politically correct) ...<P>Oh well....<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||1FST2M6||03-11-2001||09:39 AM||||there is NO resprogramming on a MSD box.. if you gat a 6A you just install it.. if you a 6AL, you cut a purple wire in side the box s comes with instructions) it still shouldn't take anyone more than an hour to install one... my 14y/o Brother/in/law can do it in an hour and a half... ||||reg||1
